              "question": "<p><span>For the ground state, the electron in the H-atom has an angular momentum <span class=\"math-tex\">\\(\\dfrac h{2\\pi}\\)</span>, according to the simple Bohr model. Angular momentum is a vector and hence there will be infinitely many orbits with the vector pointing in all possible directions. In actuality, this is not true,</span></p><table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\"><tbody><tr><td>1.</td><td><span>because the Bohr model gives incorrect values of angular momentum.</span></td></tr><tr><td>2.</td><td><span>because only one of these would have a minimum energy.</span></td></tr><tr><td>3.</td><td><span>angular momentum must be in the direction of the spin of the electron.</span></td></tr><tr><td>4.</td><td><span>because electrons go around only in horizontal orbits.</span></td></tr></tbody></table>",
